; lcse's memoff walk peels `v4 = v3 + 48` so the first load reads `v3`,
; then the nested-add rewrite mutates `v3`'s def in place — legal only when
; the memory op is `v3`'s sole user. `v4` still uses `v3` for the second
; load, but the use tally was never told the first load's operand moved to
; `v3`, so `uses(v3) == 1` passes on `v4`'s stale edge and `v3` silently
; changes meaning under a live user.
;
; Real-world hit: wgpu-core `State::flush_bindings` — three follow-on loads
; of the corrupted intermediate read `v1+v846+48` instead of `+288`,
; segfaulting in `flush_bindings_helper` during compute-pass encoding.

; test lcse
; check: v3 = iadd v9, v2
; check-not: v3 = iadd v0, v9
; check-not: v3+288

function u0:0(i64, i64) apple_aarch64 {
block0(v0: i64, v9: i64):
    v10 = iconst.i64 240
    v11 = iconst.i64 48
    v2 = iadd.i64 v0, v10
    v3 = iadd.i64 v9, v2
    v4 = iadd.i64 v3, v11
    v5 = load.i64 notrap v4
    v6 = load.i64 notrap v4
    v7 = iadd.i64 v5, v6
    store.i64 notrap v7, v0
    return
}
